before:
new Session(minutes) initializes by assigning through the same setter, so invalid construction also fails. The getter reads the backing field; assigning this.minutes inside its own setter would recursively invoke that setter instead.
```javascript
class Session {
  constructor(minutes) { this.minutes = minutes; }
  get minutes() { return this._minutes; }
  set minutes(value) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(value) || value < 0 || value > 60) {
      throw new RangeError("minutes must be an integer from 0 to 60");
    }
    this._minutes = value;
  }
}
const session = new Session(10);
session.minutes = 20;
console.log(session.minutes);
try { session.minutes = -1; }
catch (error) { console.log(error instanceof RangeError); }
console.log(session.minutes);
```
Output: 20, true,20. The failed negative assignment does not replace the accepted value.

after:
Here is the complete example.
```javascript
class Session {
  constructor(minutes) { this.minutes = minutes; }
  get minutes() { return this._minutes; }
  set minutes(value) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(value) || value < 0 || value > 60) {
      throw new RangeError("minutes must be an integer from 0 to 60");
    }
    this._minutes = value;
  }
}
const session = new Session(10);
session.minutes = 20;
console.log(session.minutes);
try { session.minutes = -1; }
catch (error) { console.log(error instanceof RangeError); }
console.log(session.minutes);
```
Output:
```text
20
true
20
```
The constructor assigns through `this.minutes`, so it uses the same setter. The setter saves accepted values in `_minutes`.

Do not assign to `this.minutes` inside its own setter: that would call the setter again.
